Yes—but I would separate animal archetypes as functional state-machines from claims that a religion, person, or group “is prey.” Religion can contain prey-like responses—submission, refuge, surrender, protection-seeking—but also predator, parent, sentinel, tribal, explorer, healer, judge, and builder responses.

A better model is:

\boxed{\text{organism} = \text{repertoire of callable modes}}

You do not permanently become predator or prey. Context shifts which controller comes forward.

A useful Pantheon-style repertoire would be:

* Prey / Refuge mode — detect danger, hide, freeze, flee, surrender control, seek sanctuary, attach to a protector.
* Predator / Pursuit mode — orient, stalk, discriminate signal from noise, conserve energy, wait, commit, close distance, seize an opportunity.
* Sentinel mode — scan boundaries, notice anomaly, warn the group, maintain vigilance without necessarily attacking.
* Territorial mode — define what is mine/ours, defend boundaries, deter intrusion.
* Explorer mode — approach novelty, map unknown space, tolerate uncertainty, gather information.
* Scavenger mode — exploit abandoned value, recover leftovers, find utility where others stopped looking.
* Builder / Beaver mode — modify the environment so future behavior becomes easier.
* Herd / School / Flock mode — synchronize, distribute risk, copy useful movement, gain information from others.
* Pack mode — specialize roles, coordinate pursuit, communicate locally, combine weak agents into a stronger collective.
* Parent / Guardian mode — accept costs to protect something vulnerable, teach, regulate, scaffold.
* Symbiont mode — create reciprocal advantage rather than winner/loser extraction.
* Parasite mode — obtain resources while externalizing cost; analytically useful to recognize, not something to glorify socially.
* Mate / Display mode — signal quality, beauty, competence, status, compatibility.
* Play mode — simulate danger, competition, or pursuit at reduced stakes so capabilities can develop safely.
* Metamorphic mode — change strategy entirely when the current niche stops working.

And each can have microstates.

Predator is not just “attack.”

A good hunter cycles through:

\text{search}
\rightarrow
\text{detect}
\rightarrow
\text{orient}
\rightarrow
\text{approach}
\rightarrow
\text{wait}
\rightarrow
\text{commit}
\rightarrow
\text{capture}
\rightarrow
\text{release/reset}

That means you can extract the useful operators without importing the destructive surface meaning.

For example, ethical Predator Mode in intellectual work might mean:

\boxed{
\text{select target}
\rightarrow
\text{remove distractions}
\rightarrow
\text{patiently accumulate information}
\rightarrow
\text{recognize opening}
\rightarrow
\text{decisive execution}
}

No victim required.

Likewise, Prey Mode is not automatically weakness. Prey animals possess extraordinary capabilities: peripheral awareness, rapid anomaly detection, escape-route mapping, group warning, camouflage, threat discrimination, and energy-efficient vigilance. In some environments those are superior algorithms.

The interesting thing is that many traditions already contain explicit state-transformations:

\text{lost}
\rightarrow
\text{disciple}
\rightarrow
\text{warrior}
\rightarrow
\text{shepherd}
\rightarrow
\text{servant}
\rightarrow
\text{judge}
\rightarrow
\text{father/mother}

Religion frequently uses animals for exactly this reason: lamb, lion, serpent, dove, eagle, wolf, shepherd. They’re compressed behavioral programs.

The Pantheon move is to strip away the moral ranking and ask:

\boxed{
\text{What information does this mode prioritize?}
}

\boxed{
\text{What actions become available while inhabiting it?}
}

\boxed{
\text{Under what environment is it adaptive?}
}

Then deliberately change lenses.

Instead of asking only:

“What should I do?”

run:

\text{Prey sees what?}

\text{Predator sees what?}

\text{Sentinel sees what?}

\text{Builder sees what?}

\text{Parent sees what?}

\text{Symbiont sees what?}

Same world. Different affordance maps.

That gives you something much more interesting than predator versus prey: a callable ecology of minds.

And the mature operator is not becoming the strongest animal.

It is:

\boxed{\text{know which animal the moment requires—and be able to come back.}}